**Entries and page fragments.** HTML pages come from directories that contain
`@entry.moth`. A bare direct top-level [$html:<code>&#91;...&#93;</code>] must fold into static page output, while
[$html:<code>~&#91;...&#93;</code>] deliberately publishes a runtime body fragment.
Direct const root `$in` inserts supply builder metadata. See @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots) for their rules.

Square brackets construct text or sequences and can compose contributions
through slots, wrappers and control flow. A non-empty text body establishes
String. A concrete receiver or formatter can establish the type before the
compiler checks a contribution.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    name = "Priya"

    greeting = [:
        Hello, [name]
    ]
]

### Head and body @#head-and-body

A colon separates the template head from its body.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    ["prefix": body]
]

The head separates each meaningful item with a comma, including directives.
Source directives use separate attachment rules. The head may contain:

- ordinary expressions, including nested constructions
- one receiving wrapper and any compatible named insert helpers
- directives
- slot declarations and insert contributions
- final `if` or `loop` control-flow suffixes

The final `if` suffix conditionally includes one body for a true `Bool` or a
present option capture. The final `loop` suffix repeats its body with an
ordinary loop header. Template control flow excludes `else`, `else if`,
`break` and `continue`. Use ordinary Moth code for richer decisions.

In a String body, authored text and nested contributions produce text. In a
sequence body, write child constructions. Formatting whitespace between them
contributes nothing. A raw word in a sequence body is an error.

Authored `.moth` templates must close with a closing square bracket.

Truncated heads, bodies, nested children and directive-argument templates are
syntax errors.

### Applying wrappers

A receiving wrapper declares slots. A bracket may apply one such wrapper.
Head output before the receiver stays outside it. Positional values or named
inserts after the receiver route into its own slots. A named insert cannot
precede a receiving wrapper in the same head.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    strong #= [$html:<strong>[$slot]</strong>]
    paragraph #= [$html:<p>[$slot]</p>]

    notice = [paragraph:[strong:Build complete]]
    optional_notice String = [if visible:[paragraph:[strong:Build complete]]]
]

The first result places a strong element inside one paragraph. In the second,
the condition governs the paragraph itself. Moving it into the innermost body
would leave the paragraph present when the message is hidden. A formatter or
named style insert can share a head with its one receiver.

### Capture and text

A template may read visible values from its surrounding scope.

Inside a template body:

- backticks are ordinary body text
- backslashes are ordinary body text
- formatter directives decide how authored text is transformed

Double-quoted string slices still use normal escaping in expression position.

### Literal delimiters in output

To render a literal open or close square bracket inside a template body, insert
a string slice:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    content = [: ["[literal]"]]
]

Use string insertion when a template needs literal square brackets.

### Compile-time and runtime forms

A completed construction produces an ordinary value. The same String or
sequence can be built at compile time or runtime when its inputs permit.
An open wrapper declares how to route and type its slots. A head with non-String
values followed by raw body text needs explicit, compatible String evidence.
The colon by itself supplies neither a receiving type nor a conversion.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    label String = [42]
    codes [Byte] = [42]
    rows [[String]] = [["Build", "Test"], ["Publish"]]
]

A String receiver formats 42 as text. A sequence receiver stores it as one
byte. A nested head preserves the inner collection. For splicing, empties
and the full inference rules, see @../collections/#collection-literals (Collection literals).

Runtime fixed binary-float interpolation and compile-time folding use the same
Moth formatter.

### MON data boundary

Unevaluated template syntax is not a MON string literal. A folded compile-time template and an evaluated runtime template are each an ordinary serialisable `String` for their producer; which side produced the text never changes document validity. There is no automatic MON interpolation. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).

### Page fragments

A direct top-level template contributes output only in `@entry.moth`.

- direct bare [$html:<code>&#91;...&#93;</code>] must be compile-time foldable and becomes a static fragment
- direct [$html:<code>~&#91;...&#93;</code>] reserves a runtime fragment slot, even if its content can fold
- a bare direct fragment with runtime-dependent content is an error rather than an implicit runtime fallback
- a direct bare [$html:<code>&#91;$in name: ...&#93;</code>] insert routes to a builder-registered destination; a runtime-marked named insert is invalid
- ordinary direct const fragments and runtime body fragments render into the page body
- an assigned or returned construction retains its resolved type
- a direct HTML fragment supplies a String receiver. Render a typed sequence explicitly before emitting it

Only a direct root insert reaches builder routing. A nested or stored insert stays
ordinary template composition. Runtime top-level named builder inserts are not
part of the contract.

A [$html:<code>&#91;$note: ...&#93;</code>] child in a template body discards its balanced body without creating a fragment. Ordinary
assigned, returned and nested templates keep their own const/runtime rules.
The old direct [$html:<code>#&#91;...&#93;</code>] entry-fragment spelling is removed.

Page assembly is owned by Project Structure. Const-template rules, including
builder slot contributions, are owned by @../constants/#const-templates (Const
templates). See @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder
slots).
